# Two-Axis Next-Generation Code Review: L0 to L2 Defects and Findings
File: openOODA/audits/nextgen/axis/l0_l2_findings.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/nextgen-2axis-review-l0-l2.md` (326 lines). Part 2 of 3.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic
placeholders. The audited tree still holds the literals.

## 3. Defects found and fixed

Table columns: ID | Defect | Where | Repair
- **D1** | Upstream commit `0620cd2` replaced the three differential-parity assertions against the canonical runtime with
  self-comparisons, and inlined a 35-line copy of the 32-capability bit table. `if packed != oo_cap_reg64_pack(…)` compares a
  value with itself and can never fail, so the parity gate was vacuous. | `openOODA-OS/oosysbus/core/capreg.oo` | Restored the
  differential assertions against `rt_cap_reg64_pack`, `rt_cap_reg64_check`, and `rt_cap_reg64_attenuate`, and dropped the
  copied table in favour of the canonical import.
- **D2** | `std/hw` moved to the `std-hw` repository, but four daemons still imported `std/hw/runtime/rt_caps_reg64.oo`, a path
  that no longer exists. | `oosysbus`, `oosession`, `oosvc-mgr`, `oovfs` | Import `std-hw/hw/runtime/rt_caps_reg64.oo`.
- **D3** | The import resolver recognised the `std/` and `oodac/` sibling prefixes but not `std-hw/`, so a `std-hw/` import
  reached the runtime path layer instead of the resolver. The shipped compiler resolves it only through an implicit path search
  that is cwd-dependent — it resolves from every directory under `/Projects` but fails with `import not found` from `/tmp`. |
  `openOODA-core/oodac/ast/load_import_parse.oo` | Treat `std-hw/` as a sibling prefix, strip the seven-character prefix when
  probing the sibling root, and include it in the `/Projects/openOODA-core` fallback that `std/` already used.
- **D4** | `scripts/link_law_gate.sh` is outside the Rule 1.14 `.sh` carve-out list, so `cli`, `mcp`, and `tui` failed their own
  `file-law` gate on every `make verify` run. | `cli`, `mcp`, `tui` | Move the gate to `qa/link_law_gate.sh`, point the CI
  workflow at the new path, and replace the blanket `.sh` ban with the normative Rule 1.14 carve-out list so the repository gate
  matches the constitution. The gate keeps every check it had: symlink and hardlink scan, link-creating Makefile recipe scan,
  workflow and script scan, and the real-ELF check on `bin/` and `dist/`.
- **D5** | The `std-hw` repository shipped without the entrypoint and package manifest that its `std` sibling carries, and with
  no `.gitignore`. | `openOODA-core/std-hw` | Add `anchor.oo`, `ooda.pkg` with `payload=anchor.oo`, and the artifact ignore
  list.
- **D6** | `std/sec/speculate.oo` called `oo_arena_mark()` and `oo_arena_reset_mark(mark)`, which are defined nowhere in the
  tree. The call was added by upstream commit `5560098` with no definition, so `oodac check` rejected the page with
  `undefined variable 'oo_arena_mark'` and every consumer of `std/core/ooda.oo` failed to build — six L1 repositories included.
  | `openOODA-core/std` | Added `std/core/arena_mark.oo` as the single owner of the arena mark contract, repaired
  `sec/speculate.oo` to use it, gave the arena cursor owner a real one-cycle restore, and exposed that restore through the
  collections anchor. See §3.1.
- **D7** | The `tui` tree did not compile: `make check` failed on 16 pages with compiler-enforced `unused import`, `unused let`,
  `use after move`, and `cannot assign to field of immutable binding` errors. The `tui` `check` target is part of `make verify`,
  so the whole repository gate was red at HEAD. | `openOODA-core/tui` | Removed the dead imports and the dead binding, derived
  the moved colour instead of moving the shared binding, and replaced mutation of an immutable parameter with the mut-local
  idiom the same file already uses. See §3.2.

### 3.1 D6 in detail — the canonical arena mark contract

The ambient arena is owned by the oodar C runtime. `oodar/core/mem/alloc.c` defines

```c
int64_t oo_arena_mark(void) { return (int64_t)g_turn_arena_offset; }
void oo_arena_reset_mark(int64_t mark) {
  if (mark >= 0 && (size_t)mark <= OO_TURN_ARENA_CAPACITY && ((size_t)mark & 15) == 0) {
    g_turn_arena_offset = (size_t)mark;
  } else if (mark <= 0) { g_turn_arena_offset = 0; }
}
```

and `oodar/oodar.h` publishes both. The runtime rule is exactly the rule the x86-64 arena emitter mirrors: keep the mark when it
is 16-byte aligned and within capacity, otherwise reset the cursor to zero.

Three facts make the upstream call unbuildable and set the shape of the repair:

1. **oodac does not expose those two runtime symbols to `.oo`.** The runtime table in `oodac/emit/llvm/ll_need_tab.oo` declares
   `oo_arena_create`, `oo_arena_alloc`, `oo_arena_attach`, `oo_arena_detach`, `oo_arena_reset`, and `oo_arena_destroy` — but not
   `oo_arena_mark` or `oo_arena_reset_mark`. `oodac check` therefore rejects both names, with or without arguments.
2. **Pure `.oo` cannot express an ambient accessor.** The grammar in `oodac/docs/ooda.ebnf` is `Program ::= { Item }` with
   `Item ::= FunctionDecl | TypeDecl | ImportDecl` — there is no top-level `let`, so there is no module-level mutable state and
   no zero-argument ambient read.
3. **Defining those names in `.oo` collides at link time.** The first repair attempt named the `.oo` function
   `oo_arena_reset_mark`, and the link failed with `multiple definition of 'oo_arena_reset_mark'` against `liboodar-core.a`.

The repair therefore gives the contract a single owner with names that cannot collide, and makes the restore real rather than
declarative:

- `std/core/arena_mark.oo` — new canonical page with `oo_arena_mark_zero`, `oo_arena_mark_valid(mark, cap)`, and
  `oo_arena_mark_restore(mark, cap)`, mirroring the published runtime rule and documenting the substrate it mirrors.
- `std/sec/speculate.oo` — the checkpoint no longer calls an ambient symbol. `oo_ckpt_new` falls back to `oo_arena_mark_zero()`
  when the caller supplies no mark, and the new `oo_ckpt_restore_mark(c)` reports the cursor a rollback restores.
- `std/core/collection/linear_arena.oo` — the arena cursor owner gains `la_reset_mark(a, mark)`, which applies the canonical
  rule to a real arena and is observable through `la_pos`.
- `std/core/collection/anchor.oo` — exposes the arena surface publicly as `collections_mod_arena_{new,pos,reset_mark}` and
  removes `linear_arena.oo` from the private-sibling list, so external callers reach it the way the collections anchor requires.
- `std/qa/probe_sec_shared.oo` — new assertions covering the mark contract (valid, unaligned, over-capacity), the checkpoint
  restore mark, and the real bump-cursor restore through the arena owner.

The published `Ckpt` API is unchanged — `oo_ckpt_new`, `oo_ckpt_commit`, `oo_ckpt_rollback`, `oo_ckpt_arena_mark`, and
`oo_ckpt_causal_root` keep their signatures — so the 86 downstream files in L4 and L7 that import `std/sec/speculate.oo` are
unaffected. Four of them, in two different layers, were checked directly as a guard: `openOODA-DE/ooclip`, `openOODA-DE/ooedit`,
`openOODA-agents/ooquota`, and `openOODA-agents/ooevolve`.

### 3.2 D7 in detail — the `tui` build

The 16 failures were four distinct compiler-enforced defects, all pre-existing at HEAD:

Table columns: Class | Pages | Repair
- `unused import` | `token/cmd_more_p1.oo`, `token/cmd_token_p1.oo`, `ui/cards/card_tool_p2.oo`,
  `ui/indicators/indicator_bar_p1.oo`, `ui/input/input_compose_p1.oo`, `ui/statusbar/statusbar_footer.oo`, `agent_turn_p1_p1.oo`
  , `theme_p2.oo` | Removed the import line the compiler named.
- `unused let 'role'` | `ui/cards/card_say_p2.oo` | Removed the dead binding. The binding was computed and never read;
  `card_say_card(theme, role, text, color)` — the function that renders the role pill — has no caller anywhere in the tree. That
  gap is reported as F5 rather than changed here, because wiring it would change what the TUI renders.
- `use after move accent` | `input.oo`, `ui/indicators/indicator_telemetry.oo`, `ui/input/input_compose_p1_p2.oo` | A theme
  colour was bound to a `let`, then moved by a later binding initialisation (`let mut ctx_color: String = accent;`) or
  assignment (`mode_color = accent;`), and then read again. The compiler's rule, established by measurement, is that **binding
  initialisation and assignment move** while **function arguments copy**. Each site now derives the colour freshly instead of
  moving the shared binding, which leaves the rendered value byte-identical.
- `cannot assign to field of immutable binding 'ts'` | `teamwork_p1.oo`, `teamwork_p1_p1.oo` | The four functions that mutated
  their `TeamState` parameter now copy into a `let mut` local first, which is the idiom the same file already uses for
  `TeamworkAgent`. The grammar has no `mut` parameter (`Parameter ::= Identifier ":" [ "&" ] Type`), so a by-value parameter can
  never be mutated in place.

