## 4. Findings reported, not fixed

These are real defects. They are reported with a remediation direction because each one exceeds a single review turn or belongs
to another owner's active work.

### F1 — L0 has no Power-of-2 shape or density gate

L0 carries 957 `MANYFN`, 336 `LONGFN`, and 5394 `DENSE` violations across 153 directories, and no L0 repository can detect them.

*Remediation.* Add the L1 and L2 gate set to the L0 `Makefile` files first, so the debt becomes visible and fails closed:

```
verify: line-cap file-law academy density vocab check shape link-law
```

Then reduce the debt from the largest directory outward. `std/` holds 116 over-dense directories; splitting on the functional
domain seams that `std/anchor.oo` already documents (`core`, `fs`, `net`, `sec`, `science`, `app`, `meta`) is the natural seam.
Split the long functions on semantic seams, never as `*_part2.oo` fragments.

### F2 — Ten L1 repositories still ship the banned clone set

Ten of sixteen L1 repositories ship `wire.oo`, `warrant.oo`, `attest.oo`, and `quorum/blackbox.oo` — 40 pages, 30 of them with
the `% 1000000007` toy modulus — plus a `persona/` subtree in three of them.

*Remediation.* Complete the RFC 0012 consolidation that upstream started during this review window. The six repositories already
consolidated show the target pattern: one canonical host per row of the ownership table, the live page importing
`std/core/ooda.oo` and the eight canonical `std/sec/*` pages, and the local clone pages deleted rather than kept.

### F3 — oodac does not expose the published arena runtime to `.oo` — *new*

`oodar/oodar.h` publishes `oo_arena_mark()` and `oo_arena_reset_mark(int64_t)` as runtime API, but
`oodac/emit/llvm/ll_need_tab.oo` declares neither, so `.oo` code cannot call them. `std/sec/speculate.oo` tried to and broke the
build. The `.oo` layers now carry their own mirror of the rule in `std/core/arena_mark.oo`, which duplicates a runtime contract
in a second place.

*Remediation.* Add the two symbols to the compiler runtime table and to the call-arity table, so the `.oo` layers can bind to
the real ambient arena instead of mirroring it:

```
if sym == "oo_arena_mark" { return "declare i64 @oo_arena_mark() nounwind"; }
if sym == "oo_arena_reset_mark" { return "declare void @oo_arena_reset_mark(i64) nounwind"; }
```

This was not done in this review because `openOODA-core/oodac` carries another agent's in-flight compiler work in the same
files, and a compiler change cannot be validated without a full self-hosted rebuild.

### F4 — `std/sec/pqc.oo` has no L1 importer — *new*

The canonical post-quantum page states that every layer imports it instead of carrying copies, and forbids toy prime moduli. No
L1 repository imports it directly; the six consolidated ones reach it only transitively through `std/core/ooda.oo`.

*Remediation.* Either import `std/sec/pqc.oo` directly from the live L1 runtime pages that need a seal or a MAC, or amend the
page's Logline so the claim matches the tree.

### F5 — `card_say_card` has no caller — *new*

`ui/cards/card_say.oo` defines `card_say_card(theme, role, text, color)`, the function that renders the role-pill header the
page's own beat 3 promises. Nothing in the tree calls it. The live path, `card_say_from_event`, computes a role and drops it.

*Remediation.* Either route `card_say_from_event` through `card_say_card` so the role pill is rendered, or delete
`card_say_card` as dead API. This is a rendering decision, so it was left to the owner rather than changed during a review.

### F6 — `oodac` `bin/oodac` was replaced by a shell wrapper during this review

The tracked compiler entry point became a 1275-byte shell wrapper, and the real ELF moved to an untracked `bin/oodac.real`. The
wrapper sets `/proc/$$/oom_score_adj` to 500, which makes the compiler the first process the kernel kills under memory pressure,
and it branches on two fleet host names.

*Remediation.* Land the wrapper and `bin/oodac.real` as one deliberate change with the ELF committed, and replace the host-name
slot table with a single configurable slot count read from the environment.

### F7 — `oosvc-mgr` kept a stale build dependency after a test rename — *resolved upstream*

Upstream commit `4c66b19` renamed `tests/test_deep_parta.oo` to `tests/test_deep_sec.oo` per House Law §1.1, but the Makefile
`SRC` list still named the old file, so `make test` failed on every run with
`No rule to make target 'tests/test_deep_parta.oo', needed by 'bin/oosvc-mgr'` and the repository's own test gate never ran.

Upstream commit `0ea041e` repaired the same line while this review was running. The repair was confirmed by re-running the gate
against it: `ALL TESTS PASSED`, 70/70 assertions. A sweep of every Makefile in all 44 repositories for source references to
files that no longer exist found no other case; the remaining hits are `*.oot` files and a guarded cross-repository probe.

### F8 — Three first-pass findings resolved upstream during the review window

Three findings from the first pass were resolved upstream while this review was running, and are recorded here so they are not
misread as still open:

- **F3 (first pass) — `oonetd` and `oosysbus` held no socket code.** Both now bind a real loopback listener through
  `tcp_conn_bind(bind, "127.0.0.1", 15353)` and fail closed with `Err("net")` when the bind fails. Upstream commits `b5f3c7b`
  and `de53c07`.
- **F4 (first pass) — `oonetd` and `oosysbus` imported no `std/sec/*` page.** Both now import the canonical
  `std/sec/{capreg,quorum,delegate,warrant,attest}.oo` on live CLI paths. Upstream commits `b5f3c7b` and `de53c07`.
- **F6 (first pass) — `std/sec/capreg.oo` carried an uncommitted working-tree change.** `std` and `std-hw` are now clean and in
  step.
